Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus
Endocrine & Metabolic Disorders
Insidious polyuria/polydipsia, Central obesity/Acanthosis nigricans, Generalized fatigue/delayed wound healing, HbA1c >= 6.5%

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: EMD-048
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of a painful, non-healing foot wound, severe generalized fatigue, and progressive visual blurring.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a rapidly worsening, infected lower extremity wound in a patient with profound systemic fatigue and visual changes. Immediate out-of-hours assessment was required to rule out rapidly spreading cellulitis or systemic sepsis, and to initiate targeted antimicrobial and metabolic interventions before standard clinics opened.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "The cut on my foot from two weeks ago is now red, swollen, and leaking pus, and I am so exhausted I can barely keep my eyes open."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a minor abrasion to the right plantar aspect of the foot sustained two weeks ago, which exhibits delayed wound healing and has acutely worsened over the last 48 hours, becoming erythematous and purulent. Concurrently, they report a progressive, insidious onset of mild to moderate polyuria and persistent polydipsia over the past six months, which they ignored. They describe profound generalized fatigue and lack of energy, hindering their ability to work. The patient notes progressive visual blurring and a new-onset symmetrical distal sensory polyneuropathy, describing a stocking-glove tingling and numbness in both feet, which likely contributed to the unrecognized initial foot trauma.
Functional Status: Limping significantly due to foot pain; functionally limited by severe systemic fatigue.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of rapidly progressive foot ulceration and deep tissue infection requiring amputation if unmanaged.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/90 mmHg | HR: 88 bpm | Temp: 37.4 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 98 percent on room air | Wt: 110 Kg
General: Overweight, fatigued, and uncomfortable when weight-bearing.
Physical Exam: Abdominal exam reveals prominent central obesity with a significantly increased waist circumference. Dermatological exam reveals acanthosis nigricans (velvety hyperpigmented plaques) on the posterior neck. The right plantar foot exhibits a 2cm ulceration with surrounding erythema, induration, and purulent exudate; however, there is no ascending lymphangitis or crepitus. Neurological exam confirms diminished sensation to 10g monofilament testing in a bilateral stocking distribution. Bedside capillary blood glucose is elevated at 16.5 mmol/L. Diagnostic tetrad features observed: Insidious polyuria/polydipsia, Central obesity/Acanthosis nigricans, Generalized fatigue/delayed wound healing, and anticipated HbA1c >= 6.5% pending formal venous pathology.
Environmental Observations: Inappropriate, tight-fitting footwear observed, contributing directly to the mechanical trauma of the insensate foot.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular prescribed medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Uncontrolled Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus presenting with an infected diabetic foot ulcer and symptomatic hyperglycaemia - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Pure Venous Stasis Ulcer - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The ulcer's location on the plantar aspect (weight-bearing area) rather than the medial malleolus, the presence of profound sensory neuropathy, and the overtly elevated blood glucose.

Systemic Sepsis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The patient remains hemodynamically stable (normal heart rate, mild fever only) and the infection currently appears localized to the immediate peri-ulcer tissue without ascending streaking.

Optic Neuritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The visual blurring is bilateral and occurring in the context of extreme osmotic shifts (BGL 16.5) rather than unilateral, painful visual loss typical of a demyelinating event.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute complication (infected foot ulcer) of severe, unrecognized Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus, driven by profound insulin resistance and chronic hyperglycaemia. Triage accurately flagged the non-healing wound and systemic fatigue. Objective assessment confirmed clinical signs of diabetes, peripheral neuropathy, and a localized tissue infection requiring immediate empirical coverage. Delaying care risked rapid progression to osteomyelitis or systemic sepsis, particularly given the compromised immune response in severe hyperglycaemia. Immediate out-of-hours evaluation was clinically mandated to initiate antibiotics, debride the superficial slough, and coordinate urgent comprehensive metabolic management, directly fulfilling urgent MBS criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Cleansed and dressed the foot ulcer with a sterile, non-adherent dressing. Prescribed oral cephalexin 500mg QID for 7 days for empirical coverage of skin flora. Ordered an urgent fasting comprehensive metabolic panel for the following morning, including HbA1c, fasting plasma glucose, and renal function.
Positive Behavioral Change: By visually inspecting the soles of your feet using a hand mirror every single night before bed (action), you actively detect minor cuts or blisters before they become infected (cause), directly preventing the development of deep, limb-threatening ulcers (effect).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to community podiatry for comprehensive diabetic foot care and offloading footwear.
DME/Safety (Optional): Crutches provided to offload the affected foot while the infection resolves.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review within 48 hours to assess the wound's response to antibiotics, review the HbA1c results, and initiate immediate oral hypoglycaemic therapy (e.g., metformin).

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the redness around the wound rapidly spreads up the leg, if the foot becomes entirely swollen and hot, or if the patient develops a high fever with shaking chills, present to the emergency department immediately.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The high blood sugar reading, nerve damage, and infected wound strongly indicate you have uncontrolled diabetes. A formal fasting blood test tomorrow is required to confirm the diagnosis and determine the severity.
Clinical Course: The foot wound will heal extremely slowly due to the high blood sugar. You require strict antibiotic adherence, strict pressure offloading, and immediate medical management to lower your blood glucose to prevent permanent eye and kidney damage.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not walk barefoot under any circumstances. Keep the dressing clean and dry. Use the crutches provided to keep your body weight entirely off the wound.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Chronically high blood sugar has damaged the nerves in your feet so you cannot feel minor injuries, and it has weakened your immune system, allowing a simple cut to become badly infected. The antibiotics will fight the local bacteria, but the wound will not close until the underlying blood sugar is medically controlled.
Actionable Steps: Take the prescribed antibiotics strictly until finished. Attend the pathology clinic strictly fasting tomorrow morning. Use the crutches for all mobility.

